  "nameStory": "The Spotted Nomad Bee gets its name from the distinctive yellow or white spots scattered across its dark body, like tiny polka dots on a bee's outfit. 'Nomad' refers to their wandering lifestyle - these bees don't build their own nests but instead roam around looking for other bees' homes to invade. They're the ultimate freeloaders of the bee world, always on the move in search of their next host.",
  "description": "The Spotted Nomad Bee is a small, wasp-like bee that measures about a quarter to half-inch long. Unlike fuzzy bumble bees, this species has a relatively smooth, shiny body that's primarily dark with bright yellow or whitish spots and markings. They look more like tiny wasps than typical bees, with a sleek build and long legs. These are cuckoo bees, meaning they don't collect pollen themselves but instead lay their eggs in other bees' nests.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Spotted Pattern",
      "description": "Look for distinctive pale yellow or white spots scattered across the dark abdomen and thorax, creating a polka-dotted appearance that catches the light",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Wasp-like Build",
      "description": "Body feels smooth and relatively hairless compared to other bees, with a sleek, streamlined shape and narrow waist like a small wasp",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Size",
      "description": "Small bee, roughly the size of your pinky nail (6-10mm), much smaller than a honey bee but similar in proportions",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Flight Pattern",
      "description": "Flies with quick, darting movements close to the ground, hovering and investigating near other bees' nest sites with nervous energy",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Antennae",
      "description": "Long, thread-like antennae that are often held forward as they investigate potential host nests, twitching constantly as they sense chemical cues",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found in areas with sandy or loose soil where their host bees (typically mining bees) create ground nests. Look for them in sunny, open areas like gardens, parks, old fields, and woodland edges with exposed earth. They're often seen hovering low over bare patches of soil or sparse vegetation where solitary ground-nesting bees are active.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Adults emerge and begin searching for host bee nests. Most active on warm, sunny days when mining bees and other ground-nesting species are also flying.",
    "summer": "Peak activity period when females are actively seeking host nests to parasitize. Look for them hovering over areas with ground-nesting bee activity.",
    "fall": "Activity winds down as adults complete their life cycle. New generation develops inside host bee cells underground.",
    "winter": "Dormant stage spent as larvae or pupae inside the cells of their host bees, developing underground until the following spring."
  },
  "funFact": "Spotted Nomad Bee larvae are master infiltrators - after the mother sneaks her egg into another bee's nest, the nomad larva will actually eat the host bee's egg or young larva before consuming all the food provisions that were meant for the host's offspring, essentially committing the perfect nursery heist.",
  "confusionNotes": "Often mistaken for small wasps due to their smooth, relatively hairless bodies and yellow-and-black coloring. Remember: nomad bees have longer, more prominent antennae than wasps and are usually seen investigating the ground near bee nests rather than hunting other insects or visiting garbage.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "Despite being called 'cuckoo bees,' these aren't related to cuckoo birds - the name comes from their similar parasitic lifestyle of laying eggs in other species' nests. This behavior is actually quite common in the bee world, with about 20% of all bee species being parasitic rather than pollen-collecting.",
  "compareHints": "Among nomad bees, the Spotted Nomad is distinguished by its particularly prominent white or pale yellow spots, which are more contrasting than the subtle markings of species like the Gracile Nomad or Black-and-yellow Nomad Bee. The spot pattern on Spotted Nomads tends to be more scattered and irregular compared to the banded patterns of Yellow-banded Nomads.",
